@* The colophon (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §11.9). One line under the wall clock, on every page, in
   both layouts: what this build is, and a link to the offer of its source.

   WHY IT IS ON EVERY PAGE rather than tucked into an "about" nobody visits. AGPL-3.0-only §13 says a
   modified version "must prominently offer all users interacting with it remotely through a computer
   network ... an opportunity to receive the Corresponding Source". This application is published so
   that other people will run it, and CONTRIBUTING.md tells them their fork "owes its users the same"
   — so the mechanism that discharges that obligation belongs in the program, set by one environment
   variable, rather than left as a page each operator has to remember to write. A footer link on every
   page is the customary reading of "prominently"; a link only a signed-in administrator can reach is
   not an offer to all users, which is also why §3.5's obligations pipeline exempts the destination.

   Note that the obligation binds whoever *modifies* the Program. An unmodified deployment of this
   tree is not obliged to offer anything. Shipping the offer anyway costs one line and means the
   operator who does modify it complies by changing RESTAURANT_SOURCE_URL to point at their fork,
   instead of discovering the requirement afterwards. There is deliberately no switch to turn this
   off: an offer with an off switch is not one, and an operator who genuinely wants it gone has the
   source and the freedom to remove it — which is the whole arrangement.

   WHY THE VERSION IS SHOWN. Two reasons, one operational and one legal. Operationally, "which build
   is this box running?" had no answer obtainable from the running instance before this. Legally, §13
   offers the Corresponding Source "of your version", and an offer that cannot name the revision is
   approximate. The usual objection — that a version number tells an attacker which advisories to try
   — does not survive contact with this project: the source is public, the tags are public, and the
   image digests are public, so concealing the number here would protect nothing and break the offer.

   No @rendermode: static SSR, like the layouts that host it. Nothing here changes after the first
   paint, and a colophon that needed a circuit would be missing from exactly the surfaces that most
   need to state what they are — a sign-in page, a paired tablet, a page rendered before a circuit has
   started. *@

<p class="app-colophon">
    <span class="app-colophon-name">MyRestaurant</span>
    <span class="app-colophon-version" title="@_informationalVersion">@_display</span>
    <a class="app-colophon-link" href="@SourceRoutes.Source">Source code (AGPL-3.0-only)</a>
</p>

@code {
    private string _display = string.Empty;
    private string _informationalVersion = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Read once from the static rather than re-derived per render: it cannot change while the
        // process lives, and the reflection behind it should not run on every page of every request.
        BuildInformation build = BuildInformation.Current;
        _display = build.Display;

        // The title attribute carries the unabbreviated stamp, so an operator reading a screenshot
        // over somebody's shoulder can get the full revision without opening the page it links to.
        _informationalVersion = build.InformationalVersion;
    }
}
